Q: A NoClassDefError occurs when loading soft dependencies in an MC Bukkit plugin A: <p>I think this may because using the method reference requires loading <code>MethodProvider</code> to construct the <code>MethodProvider::new</code> method reference and when that class is loaded, the verifier has to check whether it is correct. Sometimes, the verifier may need to load a few additional classes.</p>
<p>When <code>MethodProvider</code> is verified, it needs to check that the stack of all its methods is handled correctly (e.g. the types of &quot;variables&quot;/objects pushed to the stack match when they are used). Because of this, the calls you are doing with <code>WorldGuard.getInstance().getPlatform().getRegionContainer()</code> or similar may not be verifiable causing the <code>NoClassDefFoundError</code>.</p>
<p>To fix it, you can prevent any lambda having <code>MethodProvider</code> in its signature to be created if the necessary classes are not present. This can be done by just adding another method:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>    public void init() {
        this.methods = DependsResolver.resolveBeforeEnable(&quot;WorldGuard&quot;, this::createWorldguardProvider, EmptyProvider::new);
    }


    // This returns the interface type, not the concrete type: MethodProvider is only used if available
    private WorldguardMethods createWorldguardProvider() {
        return new MethodProvider();
    }
</code></pre>
<p>If you want to see why the method reference makes a difference (though I wouldn't recommend relying on that difference) compared to a simple lambda, you can take a look at the following simpler example:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import java.util.function.Supplier;

class Test{
        void callerMethodReference() {
                call(this::performComputation);
        }
        void callerLambda() {
                call(() -&gt; performComputation());
        }

        void call(Supplier&lt;Object&gt; supplier) {}


        String performComputation() {
                return &quot;&quot;;
        }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you compile it (<code>java Test.java</code>) and then take a look at the bytecode (<code>javap -s -v Test</code>) you can see that while the <code>callerMethodReference</code> and <code>callerLambda</code> methods are the same, the bootstrap methods (which are invoked to create the reference to the lambda the first time) are different.</p>
<p>Caller methods:</p>
<pre><code>  void callerMethodReference();
    descriptor: ()V
    flags: (0x0000)
    Code:
      stack=2, locals=1, args_size=1
         0: aload_0
         1: aload_0
         2: invokedynamic #7,  0              // InvokeDynamic #0:get:(LTest;)Ljava/util/function/Supplier;
         7: invokevirtual #11                 // Method call:(Ljava/util/function/Supplier;)V
        10: return
      LineNumberTable:
        line 5: 0
        line 6: 10

  void callerLambda();
    descriptor: ()V
    flags: (0x0000)
    Code:
      stack=2, locals=1, args_size=1
         0: aload_0
         1: aload_0
         2: invokedynamic #17,  0             // InvokeDynamic #1:get:(LTest;)Ljava/util/function/Supplier;
         7: invokevirtual #11                 // Method call:(Ljava/util/function/Supplier;)V
        10: return
      LineNumberTable:
        line 8: 0
        line 9: 10
</code></pre>
<p>Bootstrap methods:</p>
<pre><code>BootstrapMethods:
  0: #40 REF_invokeStatic java/lang/invoke/LambdaMetafactory.metafactory:(Ljava/lang/invoke/MethodHandles$Lookup;Ljava/lang/String;Ljava/lang/invoke/MethodType;Ljava/lang/invoke/MethodType;Ljava/lang/invoke/MethodHandle;Ljava/lang/invoke/MethodType;)Ljava/lang/invoke/CallSite;
    Method arguments:
      #35 ()Ljava/lang/Object;
      #36 REF_invokeVirtual Test.performComputation:()Ljava/lang/String;
      #35 ()Ljava/lang/Object;
  1: #40 REF_invokeStatic java/lang/invoke/LambdaMetafactory.metafactory:(Ljava/lang/invoke/MethodHandles$Lookup;Ljava/lang/String;Ljava/lang/invoke/MethodType;Ljava/lang/invoke/MethodType;Ljava/lang/invoke/MethodHandle;Ljava/lang/invoke/MethodType;)Ljava/lang/invoke/CallSite;
    Method arguments:
      #35 ()Ljava/lang/Object;
      #37 REF_invokeVirtual Test.lambda$callerLambda$0:()Ljava/lang/Object;
      #35 ()Ljava/lang/Object;
</code></pre>
<p>If you take a closer look at the difference, the signature within for <code>callerMethodReference</code> is different. It creates the lambda as a <code>#36 REF_invokeVirtual Test.performComputation:()Ljava/lang/String;</code> for <code>callerMethodReference</code> where the signature returns a <code>String</code> while <code>callerLambda</code> uses <code>#37 REF_invokeVirtual Test.lambda$callerLambda$0:()Ljava/lang/Object;</code> with the signature returning an <code>Object</code>. Because that information (and the call) is used when the lambda is &quot;created&quot;, the class (in your case, this is <code>MethodProvider</code>) must be loaded when the lamdba is &quot;created&quot; and not when it's called.</p>
